ure there's any other  country in the world that's dealing with  the same combination  i mean most of the other countries in  the world are supplying us with the  drugs we're the  we're the consumer here but  i i believe that there is hope i believe  that there is  that there are ways through money  laundering  um legislation and  i mean i think frankly i think that you  know the the only way  the only hope of of stopping the flow of  drugs  is to get the government out of the  business of we've been  protecting drug traffickers as assets  just as we  just as the cia created osama bin laden  the cia has been  uh shoring up drug traffic major drug  traffickers  all over the world especially in central  and south america  under the guise of fighting you know for  democracy and fighting against communism  those are the people that we the the  rationales those are the people that we  have to deal with  and the quid pro quo is then to allow  them to  uh to traffic to our nation  and i mean we saw what happened when the  faa stopped all planes immediately  it brought everything to a standstill i  think if the united states government  wanted to stop drug loads coming into  the united states all they'd have to do  was shoot down one plane  and that would really put a damper on  the whole industry  um i just want to add one thing you  remember what gandhi said when  someone said what what do you think of  western civilization and he said i think  it would be a good idea  um you know there there is no anti-drug  campaign  not a real one in this country we tell  the story of operation casablanca in  which 70  major american banks most of which i i  suppose are here in reno and you  probably bank there i know i  i do uh 70 american banks were involved  in the in the laundering of  hundreds of millions of dollars that was  an investigation suppressed at the  highest levels of the us and the mexican  governments  um it touched raw nerves  you don't have a you don't have a  multi