ty and we would tell the  story of what he didn't do  in a in the case of a murder  investigation and that took place in  in benny binion's horseshoe but um  and and certainly uh uh the greenspuns  are  our classic story uh the the offspring  do not begin to match  the the heft or the weight or the  majesty of the of the founding father  there  uh and the paper as you know as or you  may know is a  pale replica of what it once was but uh  but brian greenspun should be held  responsible for  i think the often irresponsible  journalism that he allows his newspaper  to practice and  and uh that's bad enough in its own  right we don't have to visit any of uh  any of daddy's  excesses on him  what is the attitude today of the  governing bodies of the mormon church  towards las vegas  well that's one uh door that's closed to  me i think  so i don't really know in fact my my  current book that i'm  uh just finishing is on  a massacre that happened in southern  utah in the 1800s  that the mormon church was involved a  mormon hierarchy was involved in  so we've heard absolutely nothing except  that the book  sells very well in salt lake city but  other than that we've had  absolutely no contact from anybody  this question is are you safe  and the cycle of greed and corruption  seems to feed upon itself  how do you break the cycle  i think i'll answer whether or not we're  safe let roger  break the cycle here you know i try very  hard not to think about that i've been  doing this kind of stuff for a long time  and i have three little kids and i get  very concerned from time to time  um and i i think it's just you know i  mean i'm more afraid  just kind of you know going outside of  my house with bears than i am  i mean i think i i focus my fear on  small things rather than  the the larger potential element of  danger  because it's it's what i do it's what  i've been doing for so long that if i  stopped and thought  god this is going to be dangerous then i  wouldn't do it i would be doing  something else and  i think it's just one of the the risks  of the of the job  um there certainly is no easy answer to  breaking the cycle of greed and  corruption because it is the american  way  uh it's we it's been we've been at it  here for  for well more than two centuries with  great success as you know  um it will not come alone from  grassroots popular  movements although it needs the impetus  and the  the spur of popular discontent which we  haven't even begun to  to see in this country but it will  require as the fall of the old soviet  union required  in the end extraordinarily courageous  and exceptional leadership  the soviet union did not collapse  because of popular demonstrations it  collapsed because it had a leader in in  mikhail gorbachev  who who felt that the system had to be  changed  and there were other people in the  leadership who felt it had to be changed  we don't have anything like that there  are no gorbachevs in  in american politics and until we have  one pray god until we have one  um i think that cycle will will continue  and  we he he or she could come from american  business  from the ranks of of academia could come  from any walk of american life  but uh we're going to need political  leaders  to reverse that trend and to tell us  remind us again  not of how tough or how big or how  vengeful we are but of what a decent  people we are  and that material wealth and profit and  prosperity  is not the ultimate value of human  existence  a couple questions about the whole  process of writing the book  first have the two of you collaborated  on other books  is each collaboration different how do  you divide  the writing and the research and then  closer to home what inspired you to  write  the money and the power was there a  seminal event a sudden rush of insight  or what and then finally again on  process please tell us something about  your approach to the work  when you started what was the plan how  did it evolve over time  in other words what did you think you  were going to write  how did you think you would do it and  did the plan change as it unfolded  so that's sort of a lot of questions  roll into  collaboration and the writing process  well we started uh as i said you know  when we first proposed the book to kanap  that it was kind of a germ you know an  idea about the the dark side of the  of the american system uh we went we  knew we were writing about las vegas and  we had kind of a rough  outline and um and we knew we were going  to follow the money  as from the very beginning and we really  we started at the library of congress  and we spent over a month in washington  living there  when we went to the library of congress  and we got everything every book that  had been written about  prohibition about organized crime about  the kennedy assassination about i mean  every  everything that covered every single  subject that we ended up dealing with we  were really tracing the rise of a  criminal underground  from the beginning in this country to  the to the beginning of the 21st century  the writing process was very different  we had never collaborate we collaborated  on some  small journalism pieces in new mexico  but  this was the first book that we did  together and  the process was i think quite different  from  what our publisher tells us most  co-authors do we really  we sat there we both researched  everything we were both fully  knowledgeable  about all of the subject matter and felt  that  i think roger felt that he didn't he had  to  learn a lot about nevada and las vegas  quickly  and i felt that i had to learn a lot  about the national historical  significance of events quickly so we  both felt that we needed to be  completely steeped in all  aspects of the research and when we sat  down to write we wrote it line by line  word by word which was a very  uh painstaking um endeavor  to say the least  i would have said painful  it actually was a wonderful process it's  the most wonderful literary experience  i've ever had  it's a lot more fun to argue with your  wife than it is to uh  to do this whole thing alone you know if  scott fitzgerald said uh oh there's  nothing like it it's just laying your  guts out on the table every morning  writing that is and this was really more  fun than that and i and i have to say  too that this process of learning about  nevada was really  more than that for me was learning about  a whole aspect of american history and i  thought i was rumored to be alleged by  some of my colleagues to be fairly  knowledgeable in this field  and i didn't know squat about a lot  and i learned a lot in this book and it  changed my life and my approach forever  and ever i'll never  i'll never write anything again uh  without being influenced by the  perspective that  that this book uh gave me and the book  i'm doing now  we're doing separate books but the book  i'm doing is a parallel  history of the united states and the  soviet union in the 20th century 1917 to  2000 not the cold war not their  diplomatic relations  but the internal politics on on two  sides  and uh the contrast between the pretense  of the two  capitalism and one side and socialism  and the other uh  between the pretense and the reality  disappointing on both sides  and i wouldn't i wouldn't have even  begun to take on a book like that  if i hadn't first gone to las vegas  i'm going to roll these last three  questions sort of into  one uh basically it's talk the questions  ask about whether the u.s  attorney in nevada not the attorney  general  is less eager to prosecute drug drug  dealers and other us attorneys of course  um  sally rogers may not know we just have a  new u.s attorney so i think that's  really not a  very fair question for them um but  basically the  the other two questions speak in terms  of nevadans and i think that  it could be not just nevadans but  americans  i have the courage to demand better  government  one tha